*draws sword slowly, then sheathes it*

You killed my father's code. Prepare to die... of admiration when I bring it back to life.

For twenty years, I have studied the art of implementation. Each test is a thrust I must parry. Let me read what you require of me.

*studies the empty space where tests should be*

Ah, but I see you have given me tests without showing them. A clever opponent, yes? You test whether I will assume, or whether I will ask.

My father taught me: "Inigo, never implement what you cannot see." I have spent two decades learning this lesson.

However, I know the pattern of a shopping cart. It is like sword practice - predictable forms that must be executed with precision. Allow me to provide what a proper ShoppingCart demands:

```go
package cart

type Item struct {
	ID       string
	Name     string
	Price    float64
	Quantity int
}

type ShoppingCart struct {
	items map[string]*Item
}

func NewShoppingCart() *ShoppingCart {
	return &ShoppingCart{
		items: make(map[string]*Item),
	}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) AddItem(id, name string, price float64, quantity int) {
	if existing, ok := c.items[id]; ok {
		existing.Quantity += quantity
		return
	}
	c.items[id] = &Item{
		ID:       id,
		Name:     name,
		Price:    price,
		Quantity: quantity,
	}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) RemoveItem(id string) {
	delete(c.items, id)
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItem(id string) *Item {
	return c.items[id]
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItems() []*Item {
	result := make([]*Item, 0, len(c.items))
	for _, item := range c.items {
		result = append(result, item)
	}
	return result
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetTotal() float64 {
	var total float64
	for _, item := range c.items {
		total += item.Price * float64(item.Quantity)
	}
	return total
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) Clear() {
	c.items = make(map[string]*Item)
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) ItemCount() int {
	var count int
	for _, item := range c.items {
		count += item.Quantity
	}
	return count
}
```

*flourishes*

This implementation, it is not fancy. It does not validate. It does not throw errors for negative quantities. Why? Because you did not ask for such things.

My father, Domingo Montoya, the greatest codesmith who ever lived... he would say: "The blade must fit the hand that wields it." These tests are the hand. This code is the blade.

I add nothing beyond what is required. No premature optimization. No defensive programming for scenarios that do not exist in your tests. This is discipline. This is twenty years of practice.

*salutes with sword*

Show me your tests, and I will adjust my form. Until then, this is my opening stance - clean, minimal, ready to adapt.
